{"causes":["The Warring States Period, during which scholars and officials compiled and interpreted the texts that would eventually form the Book of Rites."],"effects":["The development of Confucianism, which drew heavily upon the rituals and practices described in the Book of Rites."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"warring-states-period","sourceName":"Warring States Period","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"The scholars and officials who compiled the Book of Rites drew upon earlier texts and oral traditions from this period."},{"sourceSlug":"zhou-dynasty","sourceName":"Zhou Dynasty","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"The compilation and interpretation of the Book of Rites took place during the Warring States Period, which was a time of great turmoil and upheaval in ancient China."},{"sourceSlug":"han-dynasty","sourceName":"Han Dynasty","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"Scholars and officials during the Han Dynasty compiled and edited the Book of Rites, drawing upon earlier texts and oral traditions."},{"sourceSlug":"confucianism","sourceName":"Confucianism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"The rituals and practices described in the Book of Rites were central to the development of Confucianism, which became a dominant philosophical and cultural force in China."},{"sourceSlug":"tang-dynasty","sourceName":"Tang Dynasty","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"The Book of Rites was largely forgotten during the Tang Dynasty, which marked a period of cultural and intellectual revival in China."},{"sourceSlug":"song-dynasty","sourceName":"Song Dynasty","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"The Book of Rites was rediscovered during the Song Dynasty, when it became a central text of Confucian studies."},{"sourceSlug":"confucius","sourceName":"Confucius","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"Confucius, the founder of Confucianism, is believed to have influenced the compilation and interpretation of the Book of Rites."},{"sourceSlug":"china","sourceName":"China","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"The Book of Rites became an important source of cultural and historical knowledge for China, shaping the way in which the Chinese people understood their place in the world."}],"places":[{"name":"Zhou Dynasty","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Warring States Period","role":"Major battle"},{"name":"Han Dynasty","role":"Major battle"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Book of Rites","type":"Reference article","slug":"book-of-rites"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q862758","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Rites"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Book of Rites contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Book of Rites (Chinese: 禮記, Lǐjì, 'Records of Ritual') is one of the Five Classics (五經) of the Confucian literary canon — a collection of texts on the norms, administration, and ceremonial usages of the Zhou dynasty, compiled and edited by Han dynasty scholars (particularly Dai Sheng, fl. 1st century BCE) from earlier materials attributed to Confucius's disciples and other Zhou-period sources. Together with the Rites of Zhou (周禮) and the Rites of Etiquette and Ceremony (儀禮), it forms the Three Ritual Texts (三禮) at the heart of the Confucian conception of social and political order. The Book of Rites codifies the ritual forms (lǐ 禮) — the ceremonial practices governing all dimensions of social life from court ceremony and ancestral veneration through marriage, mourning, and hospitality — that Confucianism holds to be the foundation of harmonious social and political order.

The Book of Rites encompasses a remarkably diverse range of material: essays on the philosophy of ritual, descriptions of court and aristocratic ceremony, accounts of the ancient kings' virtuous governance, educational theory, medical knowledge, monthly calendar prescriptions, and two chapters — the 'Great Learning' (大學, Dàxué) and the 'Doctrine of the Mean' (中庸, Zhōngyōng) — that became two of the Four Books of the Neo-Confucian curriculum, the most influential educational canon in Chinese history. The 'Great Learning' articulates the Confucian programme of self-cultivation extending through family, state, and the world ('investigate things → extend knowledge → rectify the mind → cultivate the self → regulate the family → govern the state → bring peace to all under Heaven'), and the 'Doctrine of the Mean' develops the concept of zhongyong (balance, centrality, sincerity) as the foundation of moral cultivation.

As one of the Five Classics, the Book of Rites was central to the Chinese imperial examination curriculum that shaped the education of China's ruling elite from the Han through the Qing dynasties — making its philosophical framework one of the most practically consequential educational texts in world history. Its influence on Chinese social practice (funeral rites, wedding ceremonies, ancestral veneration), court protocol, and educational philosophy extended throughout East Asia.","subjects":["Chinese Philosophy","Confucianism","Classical Era","Chinese History","Ritual","East Asia","Han Dynasty","Imperial Examination"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Book of Rites had a profound impact on Chinese culture and society, shaping the development of Confucianism and influencing the way in which the Chinese people understood their place in the world.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
